  "kind": "build",
  "title": "# Icon Browser + Icon Manager (Brett, voice) ## 1.",
  "asked": "# Icon Browser + Icon Manager (Brett, voice) ## 1. Icon Browser plugin - Ship a starter library: download a couple thousand icons (open-licensed sets), stored as assets. - Views: tree, grid, list, etc. Categorized, sortable, filterable, searchable. Tree thumbnails toggle on/off. - Sources (configurable, any type): FTP/SFTP (credentials + root folder), MCP, REST API, HTTP/online, local folder, Google Drive folder, Google Photos album, Dropbox, etc. Per-type config form: credentials when needed, root/path, auth. 'Online' shows a big list of known icon repositories, each with a section for what's needed to access it (API key etc.). Secrets in the secure store. - Packs: create icon packs by drag and drop between panes. Panes are collapsible and floatable. - Tab groups: open one or many selected icons into a floating tab group, a docked tab group, or a pane. 'Open new tab group' spawns docked or floating; with a selection it holds those icons, empty it shows a + to add. Drag icons from tree to groups and group to group. - Save a pack as an icon-pack asset. ## 2. Icon Manager - Every UI and plugin gets an icon map (icon slots/tags -> icon). All of AmberX, Console, axHub, everything. - List/grid of every UI with an icon map, filterable by app. Open a map, or open a live preview of the UI and drag icons onto the preview. Save / Apply makes it permanent for that icon tag. - Global icon pack asset listing every icon in the whole system. - Inheritance: assign a pack to the whole app (root), or per UI, or per plugin. The child overrides the parent. - Save changes as a new pack: root/global pack, or a UI/plugin-scoped pack. - Icon packs are separate from design/style packs. A design pack MAY reference an icon pack but does not have to. - Switch packs globally; a randomize button reshuffles the whole icon set; generate cohesive packs that follow one theme/style. ## Rules - Everything is registry assets, with API/MCP/CLI parity and voice control. - Settings in categorized collapsible sections. - Submit UI options to UI Review first (at least 3). Watch live: Brett can watch the screen you are changing hot-render in the console. Whenever you start work on a screen or element, run `node framework/cli/axf.mjs focus <app> [route] [css-selector] --note \"<what you are changing>\"` (apps: console-web, axhub-web, amberx-droid, walkthrough-studio, demo, framework, console, axhub-android, voicevault). Agent cap: heavy steps (pnpm/npm/vite builds, docker node:22 test runs, Playwright) share a VPS-wide lock. Run each one as `node /opt/axf/deploy/../framework/cli/agent-slots.mjs heavy -- <command>` so only a few run at once. Model switches: a downgrade (e.g. Fable → Opus) is fine. Fable upgrades need no approval right now (Brett, Nov 4 2026: \"If it needs Fable, set it to be Fable\"): a step that needs Fable (schemas, asset types, contracts, engines/kernel, cross-app protocols) is set to Fable and runs on Fable; the switch is recorded on the build. Never launch a step on a higher model than it is pinned to: raise the step first.",
